  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur plan significantly departs from local optimization methods by employing a population-based evolutionary strategy that explicitly incorporates symmetries and periodic patterns observed in minimal overlap constructions. We focus on designing symmetry-reduced candidates and mixing representative initializations to escape local minima. The key improvement is leveraging hill-climbing over a multi-resolution space of fundamentally different candidate structures.\n\nThe current best performance suggests the need for modern periodic designs with complex auto-correlation properties. Existing approaches fail to systematically search for these designs. We will create a hybrid framework that combines:\n\n1. A novel \"symmetry-constrained evolution\" method\n2. A structured initialization bank\n3. Multi-resolution optimization with overlapping projections\n4. Fast objective evaluation using analytical derivatives\n\nThis addresses the challenge that periodization requires regular sampling and combinatorial selection, not continuous optimization alone.\n\nMathematicalspecification:\nThe problem minimizes the maximum shift correlation:\n    C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\nwith:\n    h(x) \u2208 [0,1] \u2200x\n    \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nDiscretization:\nUses n evaluation points (n=256 base resolution)\nWeights: h\u1d62 \u2265 0, \u2211h\u1d62 = n/2\n\nRepresentation:\nWe parameterize h using a periodic basis with symmetries:\n    h(x) = \u2211\u1d62 a\u1d62 \u03b8\u1d62(x)\nwhere \u03b8\u1d62 combines fundamental shifted-waveforms.\n\nConstraint:\n    Sum of coefficients must satisfy periodic equivalent of mean condition.\n\nObjective computation:\nUses FFT-based correlation (O(n log n)) for all shifts simultaneously\n\nImplementationprocedure:\n\nWe design four phases:\n\nPhase 1: Systematic initialization (20% budget)\n1. Generators:\n   i) Binary segments with smooth transitions\n   ii) periodic alternating maxima (\u03c0-phase)\n   iii) Flat top residual structures\n\n2. Distortion mechanisms for genetic diversity\n\nPhase 2: Evolutionary optimization (40% budget)\n1. Symmetry-preserving crossover\n2. PDE-inspired update rules\n3. Structure transfer between periodicities\n\nPhase 3: Resolution enhancement (20% budget)\n1. Super-resolution projection\n2. Resolution-adaptive gradient boosting\n3. Progressive detail injection\n\nPhase 4: Final polishing (20% budget)\n1. Multi-start local search\n2. Adaptive bandwidth optimization\n3. Successive minimalization\n\nFor each phase, we impose eviction rules based on diversity metrics and areal growth.\n\nWe use mixed-integer parameters for initial evolution but continuous relaxation later. We maintain a logarithmic archive to track optimal candidates.\n\nSearchchoicesandusefulvariations:\n\nInitializations:\n1) Phase-partitioned binary: runtime fraction 15%\n2) Wavelet-inspired: 20%\n3) Affine projections: 10%\n\nOperators:\n1) Symmetry-mapped crossover (similarity threshold 0.8)\n2) Differential mutation with periodic connectivity\n3) Local perturbation guided by Hillman potential\n\nFitness landscape:\nDefine combined coverage-based rank function:\n\n    f = 1/C\u2085 * (weight\u2081 * initial_\u03c6 + weight\u2082 * symmetry_score)\n\nTimebudgetandrobustness:\n\nn=256 resolution takes ~2\u00d710^5 function evaluations.\nEach evaluation: ~500 operations (FFT-based)\n\nFeasibilitychecks:\nRe-verify normalization at every step\nCheck objective against analytical bounds\n\nTerminationconditions:\n1) If diversity drops below 1e-4 and time < 1100s\n2) If no improvement in best C\u2085 for 10 updates\n3) Wall clock budget exhausted\n\nValidationandreturncontract:\n\n1. Check final candidate:\n    sum(h)dx == 1 (within 1e-6)\n    h values in [0,1]\n2. Recompute C\u2085 to ensure accuracy\n3. Return (h_normalized, C\u2085_value, n=256)\n4. Require at least one gradient evaluation for convergence certification",
